{"page_content": "XGBoost Notes\nPage 4\n5. Regularization - why XGBoost generalizes\nXGBoost includes several controls that make overfitting less likely.\nParameter\nWhat it controls\nTypical effect\ngamma\nMinimum loss reduction needed to make a split\nHigher -> fewer splits\nlambda\nL2 regularization on leaf weights\nHigher -> smaller leaf weights / more conservative\nalpha\nL1 regularization on leaf weights\nHigher -> encourages sparsity / stronger shrinkage\nmax_depth\nMaximum tree depth\nLower -> simpler trees\nmin_child_weight\nMinimum Hessian-weighted child mass for a split\nHigher -> more conservative splits\nsubsample\nFraction of rows sampled per tree\nLower can reduce overfitting\ncolsample_bytree\nFraction of columns sampled per tree\nAdds feature randomness; can reduce overfitting\nLearning rate vs number of trees\nA lower learning rate shrinks each tree contribution. To compensate, you generally need more boosting rounds. This is\nwhy eta and n_estimators should be thought about together.\n Often safer: smaller learning_rate + enough n_estimators\nThen use validation / early stopping to stop at the useful round.\nImportant: regularization is not one magic knob. Good performance comes from the combination of tree\ncomplexity, shrinkage, row/feature subsampling, and the stopping point.\n6. XGBoost for classification\nFor binary classification, the model ultimately produces a score that is mapped to probability with the sigmoid function:\n p = 1 / (1 + exp(-z))\nA common objective is logistic loss. XGBoost uses the corresponding gradient and Hessian values to fit the next tree.\nFor multiclass problems, a multiclass objective can be used, and the implementation handles class-wise prediction\ninternally.\nPractical note\n  For imbalanced binary classification, inspect precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, and class-specific performance\n rather than relying only on accuracy.\n Adjusting scale_pos_weight can be useful for class imbalance, but it is not a substitute for sensible evaluation and\n threshold selection.\nRegression objectives\nXGBoost also supports regression objectives such as squared-error regression. The exact loss determines the gradients\nand Hessians used during boosting.\n\nXGBoost Notes\nPage 5\n7. Hyperparameters: what to learn first\n Parameter\nStart by remembering\nn_estimators\nNumber of boosting rounds / trees\nlearning_rate\nShrinkage applied to each tree contribution\nmax_depth\nMaximum depth of a tree\nmin_child_weight\nConservativeness of splitting based on Hessian mass\nsubsample\nRows sampled for each tree\ncolsample_bytree\nFeatures sampled for each tree\ngamma\nMinimum split gain / split penalty\nreg_alpha\nL1 regularization\nreg_lambda\nL2 regularization\nobjective\nWhat loss the model is optimizing\neval_metric\nMetric used to monitor model quality\nA practical tuning order\n  First choose a sensible objective and evaluation metric.\n  Use a validation set and early stopping.\n  Tune learning_rate together with the number of trees.\n  Then control tree complexity: max_depth, min_child_weight, gamma.\n  Then consider subsample / colsample and regularization.\n \nSigns of overfitting\n  Training metric keeps improving while validation metric worsens.\n  Very deep trees or extremely large leaf capacity.\n  Too many boosting rounds with little regularization.\n \nSigns of underfitting\n  Both training and validation performance are poor.\n  Trees are too shallow or too few.\n  Learning rate is too small with too few boosting rounds.\n \n8. Simple Python / sklearn usage\n from xgboost import XGBClassifier\nmodel = XGBClassifier(\n    n_estimators=500,\n    learning_rate=0.05,\n    max_depth=4,\n    subsample=0.8,\n    colsample_bytree=0.8,\n    reg_lambda=1.0,\n    eval_metric=\"logloss\",\n    random_state=42\n)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nXGBoost Notes\nPage 6\npred = model.predict(X_test)\nEarly stopping pattern\n model.fit(\n    X_train, y_train,\n    eval_set=[(X_valid, y_valid)],\n    verbose=False\n)\n# In current XGBoost versions, early-stopping configuration can be supplied\n# through the estimator / training API depending on the version you use.\nVersion note: XGBoost APIs evolve. Check the installed version documentation when code involving early\nstopping, callbacks, or categorical handling behaves differently.\nFeature importance\n  Gain: how much the feature contributed to loss reduction in splits.\n  Weight: how often a feature was used in splits.\n  Cover: how many training examples were affected by splits using the feature.\n Do not treat built-in feature importance as proof of causality. For interpretation, consider permutation importance or SHAP\nas appropriate.\n\nXGBoost Notes\nPage 7\n9. XGBoost vs Random Forest vs Gradient Boosting\n Aspect\nRandom Forest\nGradient Boosting / XGBoost\nTraining structure\nMostly independent trees\nSequential trees\nMain idea\nBagging + averaging\nFit residual / gradient corrections\nTypical strength\nRobust baseline, good variance reduction\nOften excellent tabular predictive performance\nParallelism\nTree-level parallelism is straightforward\nInternal split / histogram work parallelized; rounds remain sequential\nMain overfitting controls\nTree depth, number of trees, feature/row sampling\nDepth, learning rate, rounds, regularization, row/feature sampling, split penalties\nAlso distinguish “GBDT” from “XGBoost”: XGBoost is a particular optimized implementation / algorithmic variant\nof gradient-boosted trees with regularization and engineering improvements.\n10. Common mistakes\n  Confusing residuals with the exact XGBoost target. Classic gradient boosting explanations often say “fit residuals.”\n XGBoost uses gradient and Hessian information from a second-order objective approximation.\n Ignoring validation. A lower training loss does not guarantee better generalization.\n  Choosing accuracy for an imbalanced dataset. Use metrics that reflect the business / ML objective.\n  Making trees too deep. Deep trees can memorize noise quickly.\n  Changing learning rate without changing number of trees. The two parameters interact.\n  Leaking information through preprocessing. Fit transformations only on the training data when using a separate\n preprocessing pipeline.\n Treating feature importance as causality. Importance means predictive contribution under the chosen model, not\n cause-and-effect.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "e7772eb60183545c", "parent_id": "e7772eb60183545c_parent_1", "parent_index": 1, "source": "data\\uploads\\37a083e744c1_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "filename": "37a083e744c1_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "chapter_or_section": "Section 2", "subject_name": "", "subject_domain": "", "token_estimate": 1563}}